Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Dust Moths Devour The Frame
- Story & Context: Young projectionist Elara, threading an unlabeled reel in the forgotten Stardust Theater, notices a faint, sweet decay like old jasmine as she threads it; the silent figures on screen begin to move with an eerie, unscripted grace, flickering just beyond the beam, making her wonder if the film itself was always haunted. "The reel spins, but the story's eyes are elsewhere."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A close-up shot of an antique film projector's gate, illuminated by a single, dusty beam of light. Inside the beam, countless motes of dust swarm and swirl, appearing like ethereal, tiny moths, while the ghostly, translucent outlines of silent film characters momentarily manifest within the flickering frame, their expressions unreadable and sorrowful. The scene is bathed in a warm, decaying sepia tone, suggesting an ancient, unsettling memory.

Track 2: Silas Blackwood's Chrysalis
- Story & Context: Old Silas Blackwood, curator of the spectral film archive beneath the Rattlesnake Canyon Saloon, confessed to tasting ash on his tongue every time he dreamt of the spectral heroine, a phantom performer who only appeared on frames of celluloid that had never truly existed; he knew his own fading memory was the chrysalis, transforming her into something both more real and impossibly distant. "I spun her from the dust and the fading light, but I can't find her now."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A lone, silhouetted figure (Silas) sits at a dust-laden grand piano in a vast, subterranean vault filled with towering stacks of film reels. A single, glowing cocoon, pulsating with a faint, internal light, rests on the piano's open lid, its silk threads seemingly woven from film strips. The vault walls appear to ripple with the spectral images of a beautiful, phantom woman, barely perceptible, as if trapped within the decaying celluloid itself.

Track 3: A Projectionist's Lament: 'The Loop Has Us Now'
- Story & Context: In the desolate town of Apparition Flats, where the wind carried the scent of ozone and dry earth, legend held that the first projectionists, driven mad by the endless, non-existent films, eventually merged with the projectors themselves, their consciousness trapped in an eternal, looping reel; they say you can still hear their lament when the town's antique cine-clock chimes thirteen, a sound like faint, rusted gears grinding. "'The Loop Has Us Now,' they sighed, fading into the celluloid grain."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An abandoned, weather-beaten Western town at twilight, viewed through a distorted, hazy lens as if seen on damaged film. A skeletal, antique film projector stands incongruously in the middle of a dusty main street, its lens glowing with an otherworldly blue light. Around it, translucent, humanoid figures composed entirely of flickering light and dust motes seem to spiral endlessly, reaching out with ethereal, desperate hands towards the viewer, trapped in a silent, mournful loop.

